WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.933
An upgrade only adds, and a
failure swaps images back.

00:00:02.933 --> 00:00:06.500
Now the day something looks
wrong and you don't know why.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.433
You can find out in minutes,
with the cheapest check first.

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:11.600
By the end you'll know
the order to check in,

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.467
the one command that starts it, and
when a restore is the last resort.

00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:18.433
Sophia's queue is empty and
Leo's sends stay drafts.

00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:20.067
He spends the afternoon in logs.

00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:22.800
The cause was one switch he
could have read in ten seconds.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.500
Order saves the afternoon.

00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:27.300
Step one, the doctor. Run gtm doctor.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.167
It prints one line per model provider,
backend, connector and sender: live,

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.900
unverified, failed,
fixture or not configured.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.367
Every line that isn't live
comes with its exact fix.

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:42.300
In the console: Integrations,
Connections, Required connections.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.733
Or ask your agent: "Run gtm doctor
and tell me what is not live".

00:00:46.733 --> 00:00:47.933
Pro move: add strict,

00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:51.767
and it exits with one when
anything you need isn't live,

00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:53.333
so scripts can gate on it.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.567
Read the lines in order. Failed
means that source is down.

00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:59.167
Fixture means a built in
sample is standing in,

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:02.467
and fixture data never
counts toward your first win.

00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:04.400
Unverified means configured
but not yet proven.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.767
And if sends stay drafts and
no approval ever appears,

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.467
check Dry Mode before anything else.

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:12.533
Ask your agent: "Why are
my sends staying drafts".

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.100
Step two, the Harness section.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.833
When gtm init runs, it stamps
three things into a file in your .

00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:21.967
claude folder: the CLI version, the
skills release and the operation list.

00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:24.500
The doctor compares them
with what's running now.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.767
If they differ, it warns that
skills and reference are stale.

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.633
The fix is gtm init with refresh.

00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:33.767
New skills come in and your edits are
kept. Restart Claude Code afterwards.

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:37.033
A project folder set up before
this check reports as unstamped,

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:38.633
which is informational only.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.967
Step three, monitors.

00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:42.867
They run in the background
on data quality, bounce rate,

00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:46.867
complaint rate and content freshness.
In the console: Operate, Monitors.

00:01:46.867 --> 00:01:50.800
Or ask your agent: "Which monitors
are failing, and is any lane paused".

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.167
A monitor can pause a lane after a spike.

00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:56.900
An approved action on a paused lane
won't run until a person resumes it.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.933
That's the point: investigate
first, then resume.

00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:01.633
Step four, follow the run.

00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:05.500
When an action didn't do what
you expected, open Operate, Runs.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.800
Every flow run keeps a
step by step journal.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.900
On the command line, gtm
logs lists recent runs,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.033
and gtm trace with a
run ID shows the steps.

00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:16.000
Errors carry a code, a
message and a remediation,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.567
and the remediation usually names
the exact fix. Read it first.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:23.633
The cost ledger links each
spend line back to the same run.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.967
Step five, who did what. In the
console: Settings, Audit log.

00:02:26.967 --> 00:02:31.267
Every operation, by a person or an
agent, sits in a hash chained log,

00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:33.033
each entry tied to the one before.

00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:36.767
For one approval, its timeline shows
the frozen payload, who proposed,

00:02:36.767 --> 00:02:39.200
which agent was involved, and who decided.

00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:43.367
If the agent says unauthorized, or
every call is refused, check the key.

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:46.133
Agent keys expire, after
ninety days by default,

00:02:46.133 --> 00:02:48.100
and exchanged tokens last minutes.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.100
A refusal on every call can
also mean the agent was killed,

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.600
or the key belongs to
another organization.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.300
A person issues a new key or
revives the agent, under Settings.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.067
One more case.

00:02:58.067 --> 00:03:02.067
An auth expired error is a connection's
sign in, such as Google or Slack,

00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:05.300
not your agent key. Reconnect
it on the Connections page.

00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:07.633
Restoring from a backup
is the last resort,

00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:10.400
for damage that something
other than an upgrade caused.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.400
As the upgrade clip said, never
restore over the live database.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.133
Rename it first, restore into a
new one, and keep your master key,

00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:20.767
because backups don't contain it.
A failed upgrade needs no restore.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.700
It already swapped the images back.

00:03:22.733 --> 00:03:23.467
Getting help.

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:27.633
The troubleshooting guide is already
in your project folder, under docs,

00:03:27.633 --> 00:03:28.500
gtmos, guides.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.267
It's written from the code, and
your agent can read it with you.

00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:35.567
When you ask your team or us for help,
send four things: the doctor output,

00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:38.967
the run I D, the approval number,
and the version from gtm version.

00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:40.000
Never paste a key.

00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:41.733
You may be thinking: when it breaks,

00:03:41.733 --> 00:03:44.333
I'll be digging through logs
at midnight. Mostly you won't.

00:03:44.333 --> 00:03:45.967
The doctor points at the line.

00:03:45.967 --> 00:03:48.100
Every error carries a
remediation that names the fix.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.767
You only go deeper when the
cheaper check tells you to.

00:03:50.833 --> 00:03:55.533
To recap: the order is the doctor, the
readiness lines, Dry Mode, monitors,

00:03:55.533 --> 00:03:57.333
runs, then the audit log.

00:03:57.333 --> 00:04:00.800
Cheapest check first, and each one
tells you whether to go deeper.

00:04:00.867 --> 00:04:01.867
That ends the series.

00:04:01.867 --> 00:04:04.933
You started with an empty folder
and agents that move fast.

00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:08.900
You finish with definitions your people
signed, engines that run on them,

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:09.600
and a record of every step.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.333
The series followed the order you'd
build in: plan, build, operate,

00:04:14.333 --> 00:04:16.967
automate, optimize, scale. An
agent asks. A person decides.

00:04:16.967 --> 00:04:18.900
Return to the clip your path needs,

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:20.800
and keep the troubleshooting guide close.
